{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Source-tool tests publish OutlineData addresses and retain mapped NodeIds\n directly in dependencies. This required Domain method is unused in\n those flows; stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is valid. It sits on an explicit function with a body, as the built-in requires. The fenced commentary is substantive, and it agrees with the code:\n\n- `Project` is a test fixture. `node_path` is a required `Domain` method, and the fixture implements it trivially.\n- In the whole `crates` tree, the only non-test caller of `Domain::node_path` is `edge_fingerprint` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs`. It reaches `node_path` only for edge targets.\n- `Project`'s `outgoing_edges` always returns an empty list. Its `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` don't call `node_path`. The source-tool tests read and publish `OutlineData` addresses and keep the mapped `NodeId`s directly in dependencies. So `node_path` is never reached in those flows.\n- The claim that other domains cover stable paths and filesystem storage also holds. Those domains are:\n  - `ReviewDomain` in `graph/validation_tests.rs`.\n  - `Application` in `tests/contracts.rs`.\n  - `StoredGraph` in `tests/support/stored_graph.rs`, used by `tests/storage.rs`.\n\n  Their `node_path` implementations are called and asserted on.\n\nThe reason is that the method is unused in this fixture's flows, not that testing it is hard. That is a substantive justification.",
